Both repartition() and coalesce() are used to change the number of partitions in a Spark DataFrame, but they come with a drastically different performance cost.
repartition() is the sledgehammer that does a full network shuffle to reorganize data perfectly, while coalesce() 
is the scalpel that only combines existing partitions locally to avoid a shuffle.

Technical Breakdown :-

repartition(n): Can increase or decrease the number of partitions.
It triggers a massive wide transformation (a shuffle), pulling data from across the entire cluster.
The advantage is that it distributes data perfectly evenly across all nodes, preventing skew.

coalesce(n): Can only decrease the number of partitions. It optimizes by moving data as little as possible,
effectively merging adjacent partitions on the exact same worker node. 
Because it skips the cross-network shuffle, it is lightning-fast, but it can leave you with highly skewed, uneven partition sizes.

Business Context :-

The primary reason we use either of these is to control output file sizes. If a pipeline writes 10,000 tiny 1KB files to an S3 bucket,
downstream BI tools will choke (the 'small file problem'). We use coalesce() right before writing to quickly condense those into a few optimal 128MB files 
without paying the heavy compute cost of a full network shuffle.

Design Decision :-

While coalesce() is faster, it can be a deadly trap. If you call .coalesce(1) on a 500GB dataset, you force all that data onto a single worker node,
instantly causing an Out-Of-Memory (OOM) crash. As a data engineer writing data out to a Lakehouse format like Apache Iceberg or Delta Lake,
I actually prefer repartition(col) based on the partition key. 
It forces an expensive shuffle, yes, but it perfectly aligns the data in memory before writing, 
resulting in pristine, perfectly sorted storage files that make downstream analytical reads exponentially faster.
